::U6L3Q01::Which substance in tobacco and many vaping products can cause dependence? {=Nicotine ~THC ~Oxygen ~Water}

::U6L3Q02::Combustion means {=burning ~cooling ~filtering ~measuring}

::U6L3Q03::Vaping devices produce {=an aerosol ~only harmless water vapor ~oxygen ~secondhand smoke only}

::U6L3Q04::Withdrawal is best described as {=symptoms after reducing or stopping a substance the body has adapted to ~proof of safe use ~a marketing method ~a type of combustion}

::U6L3Q05::Dual use means {=using cigarettes and e-cigarettes ~using two flavors ~quitting with support ~avoiding all products}

::U6L3Q06::THC is {=the main intoxicating chemical in cannabis ~a vitamin ~a tobacco filter ~an emergency medication}

::U6L3Q07::Impairment may reduce {=reaction time and judgment ~product price ~advertising exposure ~legal age}

::U6L3Q08::Potency refers to a product's {=strength or concentration ~color ~popularity ~package size}

::U6L3Q09::A perceived norm is {=a belief about how common a behavior is ~a medical diagnosis ~a drug dose ~a law}

::U6L3Q10::A target audience is {=the group a message is designed to influence ~everyone who sees a road sign ~only medical professionals ~a product ingredient}

::U6L3Q11::Why is “only vapor” misleading? {=Aerosol may contain nicotine, particles, and chemicals ~All vapor is smoke ~Nicotine is water ~Aerosol cannot enter lungs}

::U6L3Q12::Which sequence best models nicotine dependence? {=use → reward → craving or withdrawal → repeated use ~use → instant immunity → no cravings ~advertisement → oxygen → recovery ~flavor → exercise → dependence ends}

::U6L3Q13::Which is a financial consequence of repeated product use? {=Ongoing purchasing costs ~Faster reaction time ~Improved lung function ~Guaranteed popularity}

::U6L3Q14::Why does dual use not eliminate risk? {=Exposure continues through both product types ~Two products cancel each other ~Aerosol removes smoke effects ~Dependence cannot occur}

::U6L3Q15::A person feels calm after THC and plans to drive. What is the best conclusion? {=Feeling calm does not prove safe reaction time or judgment ~Calmness guarantees safe driving ~Only coordination matters ~Driving is safe if the trip is short}

::U6L3Q16::Why can delayed effects increase risk? {=A person may take more before the first amount fully affects them ~They always prevent impairment ~They lower potency to zero ~They prove a product is medical}

::U6L3Q17::Which statement correctly evaluates “natural means safe”? {=Origin alone does not determine health or safety effects ~All natural substances are harmless ~Natural products cannot impair driving ~Natural means legally approved}

::U6L3Q18::Medical use and nonmedical use are different because medical use {=occurs under qualified care for a health purpose ~guarantees no side effects ~makes every product legal everywhere ~cannot involve THC}

::U6L3Q19::Many social-media posts showing use prove that {=the behavior is visible online, not that most people use ~everyone uses ~the product is safe ~the audience is unbiased}

::U6L3Q20::Which evidence best tests “everyone at school vapes”? {=Representative prevalence data ~One influencer's post ~Package color ~A friend's guess}

::U6L3Q21::A fruit flavor in an advertisement primarily shows {=a persuasive feature, not evidence of safety ~proof of harmless ingredients ~proof of no nicotine ~a medical recommendation}

::U6L3Q22::Which response uses evidence-based reasoning? {=The aerosol contains potentially harmful substances, so “harmless vapor” is unsupported ~It looks clean, so it is safe ~My friend likes it, so risk is absent ~It is popular, so effects do not matter}

::U6L3Q23::Which pair is most directly relevant to safe driving after THC exposure? {=Attention and reaction time ~Packaging and price ~Flavor and color ~Popularity and legality}

::U6L3Q24::Cessation means {=ending substance use ~increasing potency ~combining products ~changing brands}

::U6L3Q25::Which is a protective decision connected to athletic goals? {=Avoid impaired participation and seek cessation help if needed ~Rely on a product label claim ~Use before practice to test performance ~Drive if friends approve}

::U6L3Q26::A legal product is {=not automatically harmless ~always safe for teenagers ~free of psychoactive effects ~safe for driving}

::U6L3Q27::Which conclusion avoids treating nicotine and THC as identical? {=Both can affect health and goals, but their mechanisms and specific effects differ ~They are the same chemical ~Only nicotine can affect decisions ~Neither can cause dependence}

::U6L3Q28::Which is an evidence → reasoning → conclusion chain? {=THC can slow reaction time; driving needs rapid responses; therefore do not drive after use ~The package is green; green is natural; therefore drive ~Friends approve; approval proves safety; therefore use ~The product is legal; laws erase impairment; therefore drive}

::U6L3Q29::What should a student do when a marketing claim lacks reliable health evidence? {=Withhold acceptance and check authoritative evidence ~Assume the claim is true ~Count likes as proof ~Test the product personally}

::U6L3Q30::Which statement best connects perceived norms and prevention? {=Correcting an exaggerated norm can reduce pressure and support protective choices ~Norms always equal laws ~Online visibility proves safety ~Prevention requires personal disclosure}
